<Section id="communication" title="Science communication">
  <p class="lead">
    I am very passionate about explaining science in an engaging and understandable way, and 
    I feel that many scientists fail to realise the difficulty involved in their research.
    Over the past few years, I had the chance to communicate my excitement for science in various ways:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>I won the Zürich round of <a href="https://www.famelab.ch/">FameLab 2026</a> and took part in the Swiss finals. See my corresponding talks on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KVg5Y2wq4-k">Are all European languages bad for monogamy?</a> and <a href="https://youtu.be/I9nXrWA7PJQ?t=820">What does love smell like in your language?</a></li>
    <li>I presented a <a href="/poster.pdf">poster</a> of one of our <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.02825">papers</a> to the donors of the ESOP scholarship.</li>
    <li>I got the best theoretical talk award at the Churchill College CompSci Talks series for my talk on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J8AMUNIgwKg">Multidimensional scaling</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    I am always happy to give talks to audiences of various backgrounds and ages on topics related to maths, programming, or linguistics.
  </p>
</Section>
